{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016dEavGqn6JFufke4YYLab7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven pipeline where SRDs define requirements, and a code generation agent (stitch) produces Go implementations.\n\n## Current Implementation Status\n\nLooking at the code_status in the analysis section, I need to find what's already implemented vs what needs work:\n\n**Already implemented (codestatus: implemented):**\n- rel00.0-uc001-magefiles\n\n\n- rel00.0-uc003-sys\n- rel00.0-uc005-hashutil\n- rel00.0-uc006-sizeparse\n- rel00.0-uc007-encutil\n- rel01.2-uc001-sponge\n- rel02.0-uc001-true\n- rel02.0-uc002-false\n- rel02.0-uc003-yes\n- rel02.0-uc004-basename\n- rel02.0-uc005-dirname\n\n**Not started (codestatus: not started) - everything else**\n\nThe earliest incomplete releases with \"not started\" code are in rel00.0.\n\nLooking at the incomplete work, rel00.0 has format and testutils still to start, followed by rel01.1 with cat, rel01.3 with du, rel02.1 with tee, and so on. Now I'm checking the requirement states across the SRDs to see which ones have all their requirements marked as ready. Most appear to be in ready state, though srd009-du only has a few requirements ready while others are still pending.\n\nTracing through the git history, I can see du has been actively worked on recently with multiple tasks handling different features like depth limits, filesystem boundaries, symlinks, and exclusion rules, as well as apparent size and human-readable output. The work is currently in progress.\n\nNow I'm looking at which packages have contracts defined versus which ones still need implementation work. The contract definitions cover the core utilities like sys, hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil, which already have code in place. The format and testutils packages don't have contracts yet and will need tasks created for them. I'm checking the code status details to understand what's been started and what's still pending. I'm noticing a contradiction here—testutils is marked as not started, but the implemented utilities like sponge and true have tests that depend on it, and sys is already marked as implemented. Without a source_code field in the context, I have to rely on the code_status values, but the fact that other packages are already implemented suggests testutils should exist too. I'm working through the constraints: I can only use what's in the project_context, and I need to trust source code over descriptions. The code_status shows format and testutils as \"not started,\" but that seems inconsistent since other utilities depend on them—this might be stale data from before the generation run. Looking at what's actually implemented, srd009-du has R4.1, R4.2, and R5.1 remaining, and I need to check the weight budgets from requirements.yaml to understand the task priorities, though I don't have that file's content yet. Looking at the release sequence, the earliest incomplete work is in rel00.0 with format and testutils not started, followed by rel01.1 with cat. The du task is partially done with just R4.1, R4.2, and R5.1 remaining. I need to check which SRDs have all their ready requirements listed in the requirement_states to determine what can actually be worked on next. I notice the packages that are already implemented (sys, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil) and those not yet started (format, testutils) don't appear in the requirement_states at all, which suggests their requirements might already be marked complete or aren't being tracked in this generation run. Looking at the code_status, it seems release 00.0 may have already been fully completed in a previous run, so I should verify whether I'm actually looking at the current state or if there's a disconnect between what's been done and what's being tracked.\n\nThe key constraint is that I can only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, and since format and testutils don't show up in requirement_states, they have no ready requirements to work on. Checking which SRDs actually have ready requirements listed, I see a specific set of packages across multiple releases that do have tracked requirements waiting to be addressed. Continuing through more utility commands like timeout, shuf, factor, and various text processing tools, then moving into checksum variants, encoding utilities, file operations, and permission management commands.\n\nNow I'm checking which requirements are actually tracked in the system—looks like some entries like srd001 through srd010 are marked complete or not being monitored at all, and I need to verify whether certain commands like cat and sponge have corresponding requirement IDs.\n\nFor srd009-du specifically, most requirements are done except for three that are still ready to work on, so that's where I should focus next.\n\nThen I'm looking at the upcoming tasks: tee, head, seq, and wc all have their requirements marked as ready, which means they're queued up for implementation. I'm starting to count through the requirements for each one to figure out how to batch them into tasks based on weight and complexity.\n\nFor srd009-du, I've got three remaining requirements that each have a default weight of 1, so that fits cleanly into a single task. But srd017-tee is more involved—it has 15 requirements total spread across R1 through R4, so I need to break those into multiple tasks while respecting the constraint that each task can't exceed 4 sub-requirements and the total weight can't go over 4. I'm grouping them by category and then figuring out which combinations work together, starting with R1.1 through R1.4 as the first task.\n\nWait, I'm running into a problem. If I split tee into four separate