ve been done in earlier tasks\n- R4.x might be error handling or advanced features\n- R5.x might be exit codes or signal handling\n\nFor du, I'll create a task with R4.1, R4.2, R5.1 (3 reqs, weight 3).\n\nNow for tee, which is a new command package, I can see it has R1.1-R1.5, R2.1-R2.3, R3.1-R3.4, and R4.1-R4.3 across 15 total requirements. Since tee doesn't exist yet, I don't need a separate contract task—the main function structure will serve that purpose.\n\nLet me outline the four tasks: first, modifying cmd/du for the remaining requirements; second, creating cmd/tee with the initial read and write functionality; third, continuing with tee's append and output modes; and fourth, handling tee's advanced features.\n\nBut wait—the guidelines say to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, so I should account for a tee test file. With only four task slots available, I need to be strategic about how I group the requirements.\n\nLet me reorganize: I'll finish the du implementation first, then tackle tee in three chunks covering the core functionality, additional features, and error handling. The tests would naturally fall into the next batch since I'm maxed out on tasks. Looking at the recent commits, I see that the stitch pipeline actually bundles implementation and tests together in single tasks, which conflicts with the constitution's requirement to split them. I need to follow the constitution strictly here, even though the existing pattern suggests otherwise.\n\nWith only 4 task slots available and 18 requirements to cover across du and tee, I'm going to have to be strategic about how I decompose these. Let me commit to splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks as the constitution requires, starting with du implementation covering the core requirements. I see now that the differential testing approach means implementation and tests go together—the test file is how you verify the implementation actually works. Looking back at the successful