tand what R3.1-R3.4 cover in the specification. I'm realizing I don't have the actual requirement text for the du R4 and R5 groups, so I'll need to infer what those typically entail based on common du functionality like directory traversal, disk usage summation, and various flag implementations. I need to structure the task requirements to map to the SRD specifications. Each requirement should reference the specific SRD ID and include a brief description of what needs to be implemented, though the stitch agent will need to consult the SRD itself for the full details. I'll format them consistently with id and text fields that point to the relevant requirements.\n\nFor the tee command specifically, I can break down the requirements into logical groups: core stdin/stdout/file functionality, command-line options like append and signal handling, error handling and diagnostics, and exit codes with edge cases. Now I'll write out the complete task output with proper descriptions that reference the SRD file path.\n\nLooking at the project structure, the SRDs live in `docs/specs/software-requirements/` with the naming pattern `srdNNN-name.yaml`, so du is at `srd009-du.yaml` and tee is at `srd017-tee.yaml`. Since du depends on sys and format packages, the stitch agent will need visibility into what's available in pkg/. I'm settling on four tasks: finishing du with R4.1, R4.2, and R5.1; then tackling tee's core functionality with R1.1-R1.4; followed by tee's options in R1.5 and R2.1-R2.3; and finally tee's error handling with R3.1-R3.4.\n\nNow I'm verifying the task weights all fit within the 4-point limit—each task is 4 points, so all four check out. I'm also confirming that tee doesn't have any pkg/ dependencies listed in the architecture beyond what it needs for testing and the SIGPIPE handler. With that confirmed, I'm ready to format the output using the commit message pattern I see in recent work.\n\nFor the task dependencies, du is independent, tee starts fresh as a new p