ments in R4 and R5 that I need to figure out—likely total mode and exit codes. I'll keep my descriptions generic and let the stitch agent pull the specifics from the SRD.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what each task needs: a deliverable type, the files to read and modify, what requirements to implement, design patterns to follow, and acceptance criteria. Let me structure each one carefully so the stitch agent has everything it needs. I need to extend the du implementation with the remaining design decisions and acceptance criteria, then move on to the tee task. So I'm settling on using the testutils differential testing framework against gdu, relying on pkg/sys for filesystem queries, and ensuring LC_ALL=C is set in all test cases. The acceptance criteria are straightforward—the code needs to compile, pass tests, pass vet checks, and include new differential test cases for each requirement.\n\nNow shifting to the tee implementation, which requires reading the SRD, testutils, pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling, and CLAUDE.md. I'm creating cmd/tee/main.go and cmd/tee/tee_test.go to implement the first set of tee requirements from the SRD. Now I'm moving on to implement the remaining requirements across two more tasks: R1.5 and R2.1-R2.3 in the second phase, then R3.1-R3.4 in the third phase, modifying the same main and test files.\n\nI'm realizing I need to verify whether the du and tee SRD requirements use default weights or if some have higher weights specified in the requirements file. Since I can't access that file directly, I'll assume the default weight of 1 for all requirements, though the stitch agent would handle any variations if they exist. I'm realizing the weight structure from the release notes—weight-4 requirements get their own isolated groups, while multiple requirements in a group suggests lower weights. For srd009-du, if R5.1 is weight 4, combining it with R4.1 and R4.2 would exceed the 4-requirement limit, so I'd need to split them into separate tasks. Since I'm working without the actual weights file, I'm defaulting to weight 1 per requirement, but the orchestrator would need to validate against the real weight values.\n\nLooking at the pattern from previous tasks—Task 5153 handled four requirements across two groups and Task 5152 handled four requirements in one group—both fit within the weight-4 constraint, suggesting each requirement is likely weight 1. That means R4.1, R4.2, and R5.1 total 3 in weight, which should fit together. Now I need to check the tee requirements similarly.\n\nThe tee groups break down as R1 with 5 members, R2 with 3, R3 with 4, and R4 with 3, giving 15 total requirements. If each is weight 1, that's 15 total weight across all tasks, and my 4-requirement tasks at weight 4 each work out fine with this model.\n\nI'm confident in the weight-1 assumption, so I'll finalize the output now. The ordering follows the canonical constraint—du tasks come before tee since du is from an earlier release, and within tee I'm ordering by R-group sequentially, which satisfies the requirement grouping order.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":59111,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":59111},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35126864-abf6-4b25-a9ae-8614590e1b67","uuid":"7ca05f39-d5b1-4d2b-bab9-ce8133eca625"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016dEavGqn6JFufke4YYLab7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared `pkg/` libraries (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil) support `cmd/` utilities. Each utility has a SRD, and each `cmd/` package contains `main.go` + `<name>_test.go` with differential tests.\n\n**Current state**: Generation run 44 is active. The `pkg/` packages and several utilities (sponge, true, false, yes, basename, dirname) are implemented. The du utility is partially complete — recent tasks covered R2.4-R3.3. Only R4.1, R4.2, R5.1 remain for du.\n\n**Current release priority**: rel01.3 (du — 3 requirements remaining), then rel02.1 (tee — 15 requirements, all ready).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\n1. **Finish du** (rel01.3): Only 3 requirements remain (R4.1, R4.2, R5.1). One task.\n2. **Start tee** (rel02.1): Next incomplete release. 15 requirements across 4 groups. Needs 4 tasks at 4 requirements each, but only 3 task slots remain → cover R1.1-R1.4, R1.5+R2.1-R2.3, R3.1-R3.4. The remaining R4.1-R4.3 go to the next batch.\n\n## Weight Budget Checks\n\n```\nTask 0: srd009-du R4.1 (w=1), R4.2 (w=1), R5.1 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1 = 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 1: srd017-tee R1.1 (w=1), R1.2 (w=1), R1.3 (w=1), R1.4 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1+1 = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 2: srd017-tee R1.5 (w=1), R2.1 (w=1), R2.2 (w=1), R2.3 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1+1 = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 3: srd017-tee R3.1 (w=1), R3.2 (w=1), R3.3 (w=1), R3.4 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1+1 = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n```\n\n(Weights assumed default 1 per requirement; prior du tasks 5152/51